  <!-- Guide: The IPP, demystified -->
  <dialog class="modal" id="modal-guide-ipp">
    <div class="modal__panel">
      <button class="modal__close" type="button" aria-label="Close">×</button>
      <div class="modal__eyebrow">Guide / 01</div>
      <h3 class="modal__title">The IPP, demystified</h3>
      <p class="modal__sub">What goes in, what comes out, and how to advocate inside it.</p>
      <div class="modal__body">
        <p>The Individual Program Plan (IPP) is the document that shapes the services your loved one receives from the Regional Center. It's rebuilt every year — or sooner, after any major life change — in a meeting with you, the Service Coordinator, and anyone else who knows the person well.</p>
        <h4>What it covers</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Goals — what the person is working toward this year</li>
          <li>Services authorized to support those goals</li>
          <li>Who is responsible for what, and by when</li>
          <li>Health, safety, and emergency contacts</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Where it gets stuck</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Goals copied forward from last year without revisiting them</li>
          <li>Services described in generic language no one can hold to</li>
          <li>Important voices missing from the room</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>What helps</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Bring written notes about what's changed since the last IPP</li>
          <li>Ask "how will we know it worked?" for every goal</li>
          <li>Invite the people who actually spend time with your loved one</li>
          <li>Push back on language that doesn't sound like them</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can request an IPP meeting any time — not just at the annual review. If the plan stops fitting the life, the plan needs to change.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </dialog>

  <!-- Guide: Choosing a vendor -->
  <dialog class="modal" id="modal-guide-vendor">
    <div class="modal__panel">
      <button class="modal__close" type="button" aria-label="Close">×</button>
      <div class="modal__eyebrow">Guide / 02</div>
      <h3 class="modal__title">Choosing a vendor</h3>
      <p class="modal__sub">Questions worth asking before signing any service contract.</p>
      <div class="modal__body">
        <p>Choosing a vendor is one of the biggest decisions in the IPP process. The right one feels like a partner; the wrong one feels like another system to manage.</p>
        <h4>What to ask</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>How long does your average Direct Support Professional stay?</li>
          <li>How are they trained — and on what?</li>
          <li>What's your ratio of clients to coordinators?</li>
          <li>Who answers the phone at 2 a.m.?</li>
          <li>What's your process when something goes wrong?</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>What to listen for</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Specifics, not slogans</li>
          <li>Willingness to say "I don't know, I'll find out"</li>
          <li>Stories about people, not policies</li>
          <li>Comfort with being asked hard questions</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>What to watch for</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Promises that feel too tidy</li>
          <li>A focus on filling shifts rather than fitting people</li>
          <li>Reluctance to share staff turnover numbers</li>
          <li>Pressure to sign quickly</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can always change vendors. But finding the right one the first time saves months of disruption — so trust your read of the room. If it doesn't feel like a match, it isn't.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </dialog>

  <!-- Guide: Regional Center 101 -->
  <dialog class="modal" id="modal-guide-rc">
    <div class="modal__panel">
      <button class="modal__close" type="button" aria-label="Close">×</button>
      <div class="modal__eyebrow">Guide / 03</div>
      <h3 class="modal__title">Regional Center 101</h3>
      <p class="modal__sub">Roles, timelines, and the language of eligibility.</p>
      <div class="modal__body">
        <p>California's Regional Center system is the front door to services for adults with developmental disabilities. There are 21 Regional Centers across the state, each covering a geographic area. Services are funded by the state and are free to anyone who qualifies.</p>
        <h4>Who qualifies</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>A diagnosis of intellectual disability, autism, cerebral palsy, epilepsy, or a closely related condition</li>
          <li>The condition appeared before age 18 and is expected to be lifelong</li>
          <li>The condition results in substantial functional limitations</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>How it works</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>An intake call begins the process</li>
          <li>Eligibility is determined through records review and assessment</li>
          <li>Once eligible, a Service Coordinator is assigned</li>
          <li>Services are written into the IPP and authorized by the Regional Center</li>
          <li>Vendors (like Bism) deliver the services and bill the Regional Center directly — there is no out-of-pocket cost to the family</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Typical timelines</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Intake to eligibility decision: usually 60–90 days</li>
          <li>IPP development: within 60 days of eligibility</li>
          <li>Service start: depends on vendor availability and the complexity of the plan</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The Service Coordinator is the person you'll work with most. A good one knows your situation, returns calls, and helps you navigate. If yours doesn't, you can request a change.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </dialog>

  <!-- Guide: Planning a transition -->
  <dialog class="modal" id="modal-guide-transition">
    <div class="modal__panel">
      <button class="modal__close" type="button" aria-label="Close">×</button>
      <div class="modal__eyebrow">Guide / 04</div>
      <h3 class="modal__title">Planning a transition</h3>
      <p class="modal__sub">From family home, group home, or school — what to expect.</p>
      <div class="modal__body">
        <p>Transitions are the biggest moves in adult life. Out of a family home, out of a group home, out of school — each one is its own process, and none of them happen overnight.</p>
        <h4>Start earlier than you think</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Begin conversations with the Regional Center 12–18 months before the target date</li>
          <li>Build skills first — cooking, transportation, money, self-advocacy</li>
          <li>Visit and tour potential living situations early</li>
          <li>Talk openly about what's working and what isn't in the current setting</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>What to plan for</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>The week the move actually happens</li>
          <li>The first month, when routines are still forming</li>
          <li>The first six months, when both independence and homesickness can surface</li>
          <li>The first year, when the new normal starts to settle</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>What helps</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Consistent staff who know the person before the move</li>
          <li>Routines that travel with the person, not get rebuilt from scratch</li>
          <li>A back-up plan if something doesn't fit</li>
          <li>Permission to take it slow</li>
        </ul>
        <p>There is no "right" pace for a transition. The right pace is the one that lets the person feel like they chose it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </dialog>

